  {
   "id": "browser.scroll_reveal",
   "issue": 2,
   "fixture": "browser",
   "goal": "Scroll the list to the bottom and click the Reveal button that only exists on the last row.",
   "steps": [
    {
     "call": "screenshot"
    },
    {
     "call": "scroll",
     "args": {
      "target": {
       "client": [
        260,
        340
       ]
      },
      "direction": "down",
      "amount": 40
     }
    },
    {
     "expect": {
      "path": "scrollTop",
      "gte": 1400
     }
    },
    {
     "sleep": 500
    },
    {
     "call": "left_click",
     "args": {
      "target": {
       "client": [
        64,
        420
       ]
      }
     }
    },
    {
     "expect": {
      "path": "revealed",
      "equals": true
     }
    }
   ],
   "note": "A wheel notch scrolls one row (40px) on macOS against ~47px on X11, so reaching the same bottom-of-list state takes more notches. The oracle (scrollTop >= 1400, then the Reveal button that only exists on the last row) is unchanged. Chrome publishes the scrolled layout to its accessibility tree after the DOM has already reported the new scrollTop, so the click waits for the tree to catch up; without it the point resolves to the row group instead of the button inside it."
  },
